  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis show a cut-off low \nover WY, shifting east toward Northern Plains. Further upstream \nthere is a ridge over the Pacific Northwest and a trough off the \ncoast of CA. High pressure of 1027 mb has become centered over \nnorthwestern Ontario with a cold front now south of the UP. Low \nlevel cloud cover is beginning to decrease in coverage as drier air \nfilters in and 850 mb temps marginally warm up ~1-2C, but stubborn \nscattered clouds persist in the west into tonight. This helps keep \nafternoon highs cooler than normal in the mid 50s to mid 60s. Breezy \nnortheast winds, particularly near Lake superior where gusts of 20-\n30 mph have been observed, taper off through this evening as the \npressure gradient weakens and the shallow inversion develops. This \nsupports a high swim risk along Lake Superior shores into tonight, \npossibly into early Monday morning for Marquette County. When low \nclouds clear out, mid to high level clouds take over for tonight \ncourtesy of the western cut-off low which breaks up as it moves east \nover the northern Plains. Although this is not an ideal radiative \ncooling set up, 5-10 degrees colder than normal lows are expected \nwith 30s for the interior and low to mid 40s near the Great Lakes \nshores. Patchy frost is possible (10-20% chance), but opting for no \nheadlines given limited coverage and low confidence.\n\nAlthough high pressure continues to strengthen through the middle of \nthe week over Quebec, extending ridging over the Great Lakes, the \nupper level troughing moving east over the Midwest supports mostly \ncloudy skies through Monday before clear skies return Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday night. The split flow that sets up over eastern \nCanada/CONUS helps this high pressure persist into the weekend, \npossibly keeping dry weather until early next week. That said, the \nNBM currently has low PoPs between 15-24% returning on Sunday. The \ncooler temps gradually trend to above normal by the weekend as \nsoutherly flow returns. Decreasing cloud cover and lighter winds \nyields better frost potential Monday night (20-60% chance in the \ninterior, highest west). Tuesday night could also bring patchy frost \nto the interior (10-40% chance).",
